.TH MITOS-UTILS-SECURITY 7 "September 2026" "mitos-utils 0.1.0" "MITOS Miscellany"
.SH NAME
mitos\-utils\-security \- TOCTOU hardening in mitos\-utils' recursive operations
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR rm (1)
\fB\-r\fR,
.BR chmod (1)
\fB\-R\fR, and
.BR chown (1) / chgrp (1)
\fB\-R\fR recurse through a directory tree and act on what they find.
The risk: something can change \fIbetween\fR checking an entry and
acting on it \(em e.g. swapping a directory for a symlink
mid\-traversal, redirecting a delete or permission change somewhere
never intended, potentially outside the tree entirely.
.PP
On Linux, these operations are hardened against exactly that via
.I common::safewalk
in the mitos\-utils source tree, using a check\-open\-verify pattern:
stat an entry without following symlinks, open it, then verify the
\fIopened file descriptor\fR still matches what was just checked
(refusing if not) \(em rather than trusting a re\-resolved path string
at each step.
.PP
This deliberately avoids
.B O_DIRECTORY
and
.B O_NOFOLLOW
as the mechanism, because those two flags' numeric values differ
between the x86_64 and arm64 Linux kernel uapi headers (arm64 keeps
the legacy 32\-bit ARM numbering for AArch32\-compat reasons) \(em
confirmed against a real 2024 QEMU bug report where
.B O_NOFOLLOW
was silently mistranslated to
.B O_LARGEFILE
under cross\-architecture emulation. Since mitosOS targets both
x86_64 and aarch64, hand\-rolling the wrong value would have silently
defeated the protection on one architecture with no build error to
catch it. See
.I src/common/safewalk.rs
in the mitos\-utils source tree for the full design writeup.
.SH SCOPE
This hardening is Linux\-only. Other targets fall back to a plain
path\-based recursive walk with no symlink\-race protection. This is a
deliberate scope decision, not a struct\-layout risk like some of this
crate's other FFI \(em see
.BR mitos\-utils\-compat (7).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR rm (1),
.BR chmod (1),
.BR chown (1),
.BR chgrp (1),
.BR mitos\-utils\-compat (7)
